# UniXNU-g0245d board XNU patches.
#
# SoC-level MSM8996 patches (SMP/cluster, GICv3 3-cell, BLSP, SMMU v2, UFS 2.0,
# MDP5) live in UniXNU-msm8996-common/patches/xnu/series and are pulled in via
# the "soc:patches/xnu/series" entry in board.toml's kernel.patch_series.
#
# Device-specific patches (board wiring, ADT overrides) go here as needed.
#
# G0245D/Linux build host: the Linux port of XNU's installfile calls
# futimes(fd, NULL) unconditionally, which the build sandbox refuses with
# EPERM whenever the export headers are regenerated. Ignoring the timestamp
# failure keeps the installed bytes and mode intact.
zzz-g0245d-linux-installfile-futimes.patch

# G0245D bring-up: three solid white bands painted into the firmware framebuffer
# from osfmk/arm64/start.s while the MMU is still off (stage 0 = entered
# start_first_cpu, 1 = bootstrap page tables built, 2 = about to enable the MMU).
# This board has no serial console and the tree has no arm64 framebuffer console,
# so counting the bands is the only way to see how far the kernel gets.
zzz-g0245d-early-boot-marks.patch

# G0245D v122 bring-up: post-MMU progress bands. Maps the firmware framebuffer as
# a 1GB device block at L1 index 3 of the V=P (TTBR0) table (VA 0xC3401000 ->
# PA 0x83401000, avoiding the kernel-window index-2 collision) and paints
# stage 6 (MMU transition succeeded) from start.s plus stage 7 (entered
# arm_init) from arm_init.c. [v122 hardware result: 7 bands, stage 7 never lit.]
# The cause was NOT the Apple SoC register blocks: G0245D_PAINT used `bl`
# without preserving lr, which holds the arm_init return address, so the final
# `ret` looped inside common_start instead of entering C.
zzz-g0245d-v122-postmmu-marks.patch

# G0245D v123 bring-up: (1) protect lr in G0245D_PAINT with x19, matching XNU's
# own set_nex_pg / set_mmu_ttb convention; (2) disable APPLY_TUNABLES and
# CPU_OVRD, two Apple-private system-register blocks that are UNDEFINED on
# MSM8996/Kryo; (3) add stage 8..12 sub-bands in a compact dark strip above the
# main bands so one flash tells us exactly how far the kernel gets.
# Read-out: stage 7 visible => entered arm_init (lr fix worked). If still stuck,
# the last visible sub-band names the exact instruction group that faults.
zzz-g0245d-v123-lr-fix-and-substages.patch

# G0245D v124 bring-up: v123 hardware result = all 13 bands lit (main 0..7 +
# sub 8..12), so stage 7 (arm_init entry) was reached and the lr fix worked.
# (1) Make g0245d_paint_stage rebase-safe: v122/v123 loaded the framebuffer VA
# and stride from .data via adrp+ldr, but arm_slide_rebase_and_sign_image()
# rebases .data pointers, so every post-rebase band could silently write to a
# slid, invalid VA. Load them as immediates instead.
# (2) Add a third strip, index 13..21 (row 700 + 32*(i-13), 16 rows, pitch 32
# deliberately distinct from 64/40 so the three groups separate by gap ratio),
# and paint nine marks inside arm_init(): before/after slide+rebase+sign,
# cpu_data_init, PE_init_platform(FALSE), before/after configure_misc_apple_regs,
# thread_bootstrap, PE_init_printf, and just before machine_startup.
# No functional changes: one patch series step verifies one hypothesis.
zzz-g0245d-v124-arm-init-substages.patch

