# v129: v128 photo -> K=3 (third strip slots 13/14/15 lit 0.99/0.90/0.86,
# 16..21 dark 0.04~0.16). So the kernel DOES run: it dies between
# cpu_bootstrap() (band 15) and kernel_startup_bootstrap()'s return (band 16).
# arm_init's own 16..21 marks are unreachable, so relocating them into
# startup.c is conflict-free. This patch only touches osfmk/kern/startup.c:
#   16 = entered kernel_startup_bootstrap()
#   17 = n/size checks passed (n should be ~4006, section __init_entry_set 96KB)
#   18 = qsort() of 4006 entries returned
#   19 = entered kernel_startup_initialize_upto() (assert passed)
#   20 = first entry of subsystem 2 (TIMEOUTS)
#   21 = first entry of subsystem 3 (LOCKS)
# Read-out: K=4 died before qsort; K=5 died in qsort; K=6 died before/during
# the initialize_upto call (assert?); K=7 died in TUNABLES; K=8 died in
# TIMEOUTS; K=9 died in LOCKS (or completed). rtclock_early_init() is nearly
# empty on arm (only PE_register_timebase_callback), so it is not a suspect.
zzz-g0245d-v129-startup-bootstrap-marks.patch
# v130: v129 photo -> K=3 again, but v129's band 16 is painted at the ENTRY of
# kernel_startup_bootstrap(); it stayed dark, so the kernel dies BEFORE entering
# it. The gap between band 15 (after cpu_bootstrap) and that entry is only:
#     rtclock_early_init();
#     kernel_debug_string_early("kernel_startup_bootstrap");
#     kernel_startup_bootstrap();
# rtclock_early_init() (osfmk/arm/rtclock.c) is one call to
# PE_register_timebase_callback(timebase_callback), whose callback PANICS when
# the timebase constants look invalid - and a panic here has no console, so it
# looks like a silent hang. DTS does carry timebase-frequency = 19200000, so the
# constants can still be wrong for another reason.
# This patch only re-assigns the two low slots:
#   16 = rtclock_early_init() returned      (arm_init.c)
#   17 = kernel_debug_string_early() returned, about to enter bootstrap
# and removes v129's 16/17 marks inside kernel_startup_bootstrap() (18 qsort /
# 19 initialize_upto entry / 20 TIMEOUTS / 21 LOCKS unchanged).
# Read-out: K=4 => died INSIDE rtclock_early_init() (timebase callback panic);
# K=5 => died in kernel_debug_string_early() or at bootstrap entry;
# K=6 => died inside kernel_startup_bootstrap() before qsort.
zzz-g0245d-v130-rtclock-marks.patch
# v131: ROOT CAUSE of the band-16 blackout. v130 placed band 16 right after
# rtclock_early_init(); it stayed dark -> the kernel dies INSIDE that function.
# rtclock_early_init() (osfmk/arm/rtclock.c) calls
# PE_register_timebase_callback(timebase_callback); PE_register_timebase_callback
# (pexpert/arm/pe_init.c) unconditionally calls PE_call_timebase_callback(), which
# fills num = gPEClockFrequencyInfo.timebase_frequency_hz, den = 1. timebase_callback
# then does: if (den < 1 || den > 4 || num < den) panic("rtclock timebase_callback:
# invalid constant") - and a panic this early has no console, so it looks like a
# silent hang. gPEClockFrequencyInfo is all zero because pe_identify_machine()
# bails out at its very first statement:
#     if (pe_arm_get_soc_base_phys() == 0) return;
# and pe_arm_get_soc_base_phys() only succeeds when the DT has a node with
# name="arm-io" - an Apple-ism. This board's DT (hardware.dtb + make-adt.py) has
# no arm-io, so it returns 0, the defaults (timebase 24 MHz) are never installed,
# and the callback sees num=0/den=1 -> panic. Removing that early return lets the
# defaults apply; the /cpus loop finds no "state" property on the cpu nodes and
# simply leaves 24 MHz in place. v130's bands are kept so we can watch the next
# milestone light up (16 = rtclock returned).
zzz-g0245d-v131-no-armio-early-return.patch
# v132: v131 photo -> K=9, the WHOLE third strip lit. The arm-io early-return fix
# worked: the kernel now clears rtclock_early_init(), kernel_startup_bootstrap(),
# qsort() and kernel_startup_initialize_upto(LOCKS) - the old "death near arm_init
# stage 19" is gone. BUT the probe indices had become ambiguous: v128 left marks
# 17..21 in arm_init (processor_bootstrap / arm_vm_init / serial_init /
# PE_create_console / end) while v129 put 18..21 inside startup.c, so "9 lit" could
# not tell WHICH painter fired. This patch rebuilds a single clean monotonic ladder
# in arm_init.c and removes every startup.c mark:
#   13 thread_bootstrap returned      14 cpu_bootstrap returned
#   15 rtclock_early_init returned    16 kernel_debug_string_early returned
#   17 kernel_startup_bootstrap returned
#   18 processor_bootstrap returned   19 arm_vm_init returned (page-table boundary!)
#   20 serial_init returned           21 arm_init end, just before machine_startup()
# If 19 is lit but 20 is dark the bootstrap V=P framebuffer mapping survived
# arm_vm_init(); if 19 is dark the kernel died at the page-table switch.
zzz-g0245d-v132-arm-init-ladder.patch
# v133: v132 photo -> K=6 (13..18 lit, 19..21 dark): the kernel stops at
# arm_vm_init(). Reading osfmk/arm64/arm_vm_init.c explains it:
#     arm_vm_physmap_init(args);
#     set_mmu_ttb_alternate(cpu_ttep & TTBR_BADDR_MASK);
#     set_mmu_ttb(invalid_ttep & TTBR_BADDR_MASK);   /* TTBR0 -> invalid */
#     flush_mmu_tlb(); kva_active = TRUE;
#     /* From here on out, we're off the bootstrap translation tables. */
# Our framebuffer VA 0xC3401000 is a LOW address, i.e. TTBR0 space, valid only in
# the bootstrap V=P table. So the paint() right after arm_vm_init() writes an
# unmapped VA and faults - the "hang at arm_vm_init" may be OUR probe killing the
# kernel, not a real bug. This patch keeps arm_init.c as the only file changed
# (arm_vm_init.c is already modified by xnu-12377/non-apple-cache-monitor.patch, so
# a hunk anchored there does not apply) and routes the three post-VM marks through
# the kernel physmap instead of the dead bootstrap VA:
#   19 = arm_vm_init() returned      20 = serial_init() returned
#   21 = arm_init() end
# using   va = gVirtBase + (0x83401000 - gPhysBase)   (the classic phystokv form).
# Read-out: 19 lights => arm_vm_init really completed and the kernel runs on the
# real kernel tables; 19 dark => either it dies there for real, or the physmap does
# not cover the framebuffer (then we need ml_io_map instead).
zzz-g0245d-v133-post-vm-physmap-paints.patch
# v134: v133 (physmap painter) still showed K=6, i.e. band 19 stayed dark. Two
# possibilities remain and they look identical on screen:
#   (a) arm_vm_init() really dies, or
#   (b) the physmap VA (gVirtBase + 0x83401000 - gPhysBase) is not writable and
#       OUR paint faults.
# The DT says the framebuffer sits at 0x83401000 inside reserved-memory under a
# 4 GB /memory@80000000 node, so the physmap *should* cover it - which pushes the
# balance toward (a). To settle it, put two marks INSIDE arm_vm_init() using the
# OLD bootstrap-VA painter, which is still valid up to the TTBR switch:
#     19 = just before arm_set_user_tbi()   (main body done, tables built)
#     20 = immediately before set_mmu_ttb_alternate()
# Read-out: 19 dark => died early in arm_vm_init; 19 lit / 20 dark => died between;
# 19+20 lit => the function reached the switch, so the earlier dark 19 was our
# physmap paint failing, not arm_vm_init.
# NOTE: this file is already patched by xnu-12377 patches, so the hunk is generated
# against a reconstructed tree (device patches applied first), not the raw source.
zzz-g0245d-v134-arm-vm-init-inner-marks.patch
# v135: v134 photo still K=6. BOTH probes were inside arm_vm_init() and used the
# bootstrap-VA painter, which is valid there, so band 19 (just before
# arm_set_user_tbi()) staying dark means arm_vm_init() really dies somewhere in
# its body - this is a genuine bug, not a probe artefact.
# Ruled out: BOOTSTRAP_TABLE_SIZE. native-4k-bootstrap-capacity.patch IS applied
# (16 pages for native 4K; 4 GB needs 2*(1+ceil(4G/1G))+2 = 12) so the
# "too small for memory config" panic cannot fire.
# This patch bisects the function body with one extra early mark, and drops
# arm_init's 19/20/21 (physmap painter) so the indices cannot collide:
#     21 = after gPhysSize/mem_size are computed   (first ~20 statements)
#     19 = just before arm_set_user_tbi()          (late body)
#     20 = just before set_mmu_ttb_alternate()     (the TTBR switch)
# Read-out: 21 dark => dies in the first statements; 21 lit/19 dark => dies in the
# page-table construction in between; 19 lit/20 dark => dies right at the switch.
zzz-g0245d-v135-arm-vm-init-bisect.patch
# v136: v135 photo -> K=6 again, and v135's earliest mark (21, six statements into
# arm_vm_init()) was ALSO dark. That squeezes the death into a tiny window:
#     processor_bootstrap();      -> band 18 lit
#     PE_parse_boot_argn("maxmem", ...)
#     arm_vm_init(xmaxmem, args); -> its first 4 assignments, then band 21
# so either the maxmem parse kills us, or the kernel never really enters
# arm_vm_init. This patch moves the mark to the CALL SITE (arm_init.c, immediately
# before arm_vm_init()) and drops the in-function early one, so the answer is binary:
#     21 lit  => we reach the call; the death is inside arm_vm_init's first lines
#     21 dark => we die before the call (maxmem parsing / the call itself)
# 19 (before arm_set_user_tbi) and 20 (before the TTBR switch) are kept as-is.
zzz-g0245d-v136-callsite-mark.patch
# v137: v136 photo -> K=6 again, so band 21 AT THE CALL SITE stayed dark: the
# kernel dies between processor_bootstrap() (band 18) and arm_vm_init().
# That window is not just the maxmem parse, it contains:
#     PE_parse_boot_argn("maxmem", &maxmem, ...) / PE_get_default("hw.memsize", ...)
#     ... SCHED_HYGIENE_DEBUG block ...
#     nanoseconds_to_absolutetime(XCALL_ACK_TIMEOUT_NS, ...)
#     HAS_BP_RET: PE_parse_boot_argn("bpret") + set_bp_ret()
#     PE_parse_boot_argn("immediate_NMI", ...)
#     __builtin_arm_wsr("pan", 1)   (PAN)
# Two real defects found in that window (both fixed here):
#   1) PE_get_default() has NO /defaults node in this board's ADT (hardware.dtb and
#      make-adt.py have neither /defaults nor /chosen), so it always falls through to
#      IODTGetDefault() -> IORegistryEntry::fromPath("/defaults", gIODTPlane): a call
#      into the IOKit registry from arm_init(), long before IOKit is up.
#   2) "memsize" is an uninitialised uint32_t local, so if that call ever returned
#      TRUE the kernel would do xmaxmem = <stack garbage>, and arm_vm_init() CLAMPS
#      mem_size with it:  if (memory_size != 0 && mem_size > memory_size)
#                             mem_size = memory_size;
#      A small garbage value would shrink the whole physmap/bootstrapping geometry.
# So: drop the PE_get_default branch (default xmaxmem = 0 -> use args->memSize) and
# initialise memsize. Three marks now bisect the window:
#     19 = maxmem resolution done
#     20 = nanoseconds_to_absolutetime() done
#     21 = at the arm_vm_init() call site
# arm_vm_init.c's old 19/20 are removed so indices cannot collide.
# Read-out: 19 dark => dies inside PE_parse_boot_argn; 19 lit/20 dark => dies in the
# sched-hygiene/bpret/NMI/PAN group or in nanoseconds_to_absolutetime; 20 lit/21 dark
# => dies in the last few statements; 21 lit => the window is cleared and the earlier
# dark 19 was PE_get_default itself.
zzz-g0245d-v137-maxmem-path.patch
# v138: v137 photo -> K=6, i.e. the window between band 18 (processor_bootstrap)
# and band 21 (arm_vm_init call site) still kills the kernel. The v137 patch had
# already removed the two real defects found there (PE_get_default("hw.memsize")
# without a /defaults node, and the uninitialised memsize local), so what is left
# in that window is the PAN sequence: arch/configs/common.config sets
# CONFIG_XNU_NATIVE_PAN=y, native-arm-pan.patch then defines
# __ARM_PAN_AVAILABLE__=1 in BCM2837.h and arm_init.c executes
# __builtin_arm_wsr("pan", 1). PAN is ARMv8.1+; MSM8996 Kryo is ARMv8.0, so this
# is an undefined instruction - a silent freeze with no console. v138 is that
# config change only (# CONFIG_XNU_NATIVE_PAN is not set in
# configs/g0245d_defconfig), no patch file needed.
# Hardware result (2026-11-13): the kernel no longer freezes; the BootShim log is
# complete, 4 inputs validate, boot services exit - and then the screen goes
# BLACK. So the PAN root cause is confirmed and XNU runs well past arm_init().
# v139 hardware result: the stage-24 WHITE BAND was visible at 45% screen
# height (PE_create_console returned), but the flat blue fill was overwritten
# by the vc console with a mismatched pixel format - magenta grid texture.
# A colour fill dies to any console rewrite; an all-0xFF band reads white
# under every pixel format. So v140 returns to the v137 band geometry
# (rowbytes 0xCA8) with the band ladder extended to 26..34:
#   26 PE_init_printf   27 cpu_machine_idle_init  28 PE_init_platform
#   29 ml_map_cpu_pio   30 cpu_timebase_init      31 kptimer_curcpu_up
#   32 PE_init_cpu      33 fiq_context_init       34 DT dram-base/size
#
# G0245D v141 (hardware 2026-11-14): v140's physmap band repaint (mark_vm ->
# g0245d_paint_stage_vm) faults the first time it runs, right after arm_vm_init.
# Photo read-out: bands 13..21 lit, 22..34 all dark, and NO v139-like magenta
# console -> it died before PE_create_console, i.e. EARLIER than v139. So v141
# drops v140's mark_vm/mark_va repaint entirely and instead leans on the vc
# console log. The boot shim reports width=1000 but rowBytes=3240 (a 32bpp line
# is 810 px), so the console overruns every row and smears into a pattern; v141
# recomputes v_width = rowBytes/(depth>>3) so the console matches the stride.
zzz-g0245d-v141-console-width.patch

# G0245D v142 (hardware 2026-11-14): with v141 the screen briefly shows the
# bootloader + white bands 13..21, then goes BLACK. Black = the console init /
# clear ran (so arm_vm_init + serial_init + PE_create_console all returned), but
# no text is emitted -> it dies right after PE_create_console, before/at
# PE_init_printf (same region as v139's 24<->25 death). To see exactly WHERE it
# dies after the console, paint the bands through the console's OWN mapped fb VA
# (vinfo.v_baseaddr - the VA the clear uses, guaranteed mapped), NOT the physmap
# VA v140 tried (which faulted). g0245d_vc_paint() lives in video_console.c and
# is called after each post-console step; it repaints 13..index so console clears
# between steps stay visible. Highest lit band = the last step completed.
# DROPPED in v143: console text now carries the progress; these white bands
# would blank out half the text rows and fight the log. Kept on disk for archive.
# zzz-g0245d-v142-postconsole-vc-bands.patch

# G0245D v143 (hardware 2026-11-14): the root cause of the "bands then black"
# screens. In arm_init() the boot args carry serial=3 (SERIALMODE_OUTPUT) and
# serial_init() enumerated a UART, so the kernel calls switch_to_serial_console()
# BEFORE PE_create_console(). kprintf is then routed to a ttyHSL0 that has no
# physical connector, and PE_create_console() clears the fb to black - so the
# screen is black with NO output even though the kernel is still running on the
# serial path. v143 forces the console back onto the video fb right after
# PE_create_console() so the boot log prints to the screen and the real death
# point becomes visible.
zzz-g0245d-v143-force-video-console.patch

# G0245D v144 (hardware 2026-11-14): the real reason every console write killed
# the screen. PE_create_console does NOT clear; instead vc_initialize() decides
# whether to ml_io_map_unmappable() the framebuffer from the boot shim's
# v_baseAddr. The shim hands us the RAW PHYSICAL address 0x83401000 with bit0
# clear, so makeMapping stays FALSE and the console uses that physical address
# as a kernel VA. The first console write (kprintf, or v142's band paint) then
# faults -> panic -> black. v144 treats any address below the kernel-VA range as
# physical and forces the mapping. With a valid fb VA and v143 forcing the video
# console, the boot log should finally print to the screen.
zzz-g0245d-v144-force-fb-map.patch

# G0245D v145 (hardware 2026-11-14): v144 (forced fb mapping) still showed only
# "bands then black", so the console-vs-fb theory is not confirmed. Decisive
# panel test: right before arm_vm_init (bootstrap VA 0xC3401000 still valid per
# start.s), fill the WHOLE framebuffer solid white (3240*1920 bytes). Reads:
#   - whole screen stays bright white -> panel/backlight is still ON after
#     arm_vm_init; the black was fb content / console routing (fixable in sw).
#   - goes black -> the display/backlight itself is switched off around
#     arm_vm_init (hardware/clock/DSI), and fb writes can never show: need a
#     real display init / keep the panel powered, not more fb writes.
# v145 (fullscreen white) + v146 (white+halt) were DIAGNOSTIC: they proved the
# display works (v146 showed a pure-white screen). Now that 24bpp is the fix,
# they are disabled so the console can run on the real boot path.
# zzz-g0245d-v145-fullscreen-white.patch
# zzz-g0245d-v146-white-halt.patch

# G0245D v147 (hardware 2026-11-14): THE display fix. v146 (full-white fill +
# halt) proved the panel/MDP5/fb-write path all work (screen went pure white), so
# the "black screen" was never hardware: the ft8716 panel is 24bpp RGB888 but the
# XNU vc console only has 8/16/30/32 paths, so it painted a 32bpp black
# background over the framebuffer. v147 adds a real 24bpp path to the video
# console (vc_render_char case 24, vc_paint_char_24 at 3 bytes/pixel, dispatch)
# and forces v_depth=24 + v_width=rowBytes/3=1080 in pe_init. White-on-black is
# byte-order agnostic, so the boot log should finally render on the panel.
zzz-g0245d-v147-console-24bpp.patch

# G0245D v148 (hardware 2026-11-14): v147 also showed "bands flash then black".
# Root cause of the black: machine_startup calls initialize_screen(NULL,
# kPEAcquireScreen), which (a) runs vc_progress_set() -> wipes the framebuffer
# to the progress background and (b) runs gc_enable(FALSE) -> because the
# console is not serial, that sets disableConsoleOutput = TRUE. Net effect: the
# panel goes black and NOT ONE character of kernel output is ever painted, so
# every version since v141 looked dead while the kernel kept running. That is
# also why v146 (halted before arm_vm_init) stayed white and v145 ("no white")
# did not: the fill was wiped by the progress screen.
# Fix: right after kPEAcquireScreen, switch_to_video_console() +
# initialize_screen(NULL, kPETextScreen) to bring the text console back, and
# make kernel_bootstrap_log() print unconditionally so the bootstrap trace
# names every subsystem the kernel enters.
zzz-g0245d-v148-text-console.patch

# G0245D v149 (hardware 2026-11-14): v148 still showed no text. The text console
# needs gc_enabled + a rendered font + the right pixel format +
# disableConsoleOutput == FALSE; any one failing makes a *live* kernel look like
# a dead black panel, and we cannot tell those apart from a photo. This patch
# adds a console-independent marker: g0245d_fb_mark(n) writes straight to
# vinfo.v_baseaddr and memsets the FULL stride (v_rowbytes) to 0xFF, so the band
# is white regardless of v_depth / v_width / fonts. Bands are cumulative
# (mark(n) paints 1..n), so the number of white bars = highest mark reached:
#   1 = entered initialize_screen with a mapped framebuffer (PE_create_console)
#   2 = entered kernel_bootstrap_thread, before kPEAcquireScreen
#   3 = survived kPEAcquireScreen + the text-console restore
#   4 = survived vm_commpage_init / vm_commpage_text_init
zzz-g0245d-v149-fb-marks.patch

# G0245D v150 (hardware 2026-11-14): the v149 marks did not show on the photo,
# but the display is vertically flipped (fb row 0 = bottom of the panel) and
# v149 put them at fb rows 40..244, i.e. the last few percent of the panel, which
# the photo cropped off. Move them to fb rows 1000..1360 (an area with no
# pre-existing stripes) and raise the count to 5:
#   1 = initialize_screen reached with a mapped framebuffer (PE_create_console)
#   2 = kernel_bootstrap_thread, right before PE_init_iokit
#   3 = just before kPEAcquireScreen (the call that clears the screen)
#   4 = survived kPEAcquireScreen + text-console restore
#   5 = survived vm_commpage_init / vm_commpage_text_init
zzz-g0245d-v150-marks-visible.patch
